Are you sure it's a plain old alignment problem? If it were, you'd think
there'd be differences in other struct sizes, but only the you struct
has a different size.

I think VC++ pads out bitfields to 32 bits, while bcc is happy with
padding out to 8 bits. I could be wildly wrong, of course.

Anyway, to the OP, if you can't work out how to get bcc to play ball,
you can always use gcc. MinGW is easy to install and use.

Alternatively, you can probably use bcc to produce a binary that's
compatible with the official DOS binary (which is compiled with gcc,
iirc) and exchange bones with DOS users. I think all you need to do is
to compile with the MAIL option.

